description:
src/worker/mod.rs resolve_adapter() (line ~2812) silently falls back to the claude-sonnet built-in when the configured adapter (config.agent.default) is not found in the dispatcher's loaded adapter map. This causes workers to dispatch beads with claude-sonnet — which uses 'unbuffer -p claude' and triggers the PTY escape-sequence transform failure — instead of failing with a clear error.

## Root cause

When a worker starts with --agent claude-code-glm-47, if the adapter file is not found or not loaded into the dispatcher, resolve_adapter falls through to:

  if let Some(adapter) = self.dispatcher.adapter("claude-sonnet") {
      tracing::warn!(..., "configured adapter not found, using fallback");
      return Ok(adapter.clone());
  }

The warn log is never surfaced to the user and the worker silently degrades.

## Fix

Remove the claude-sonnet fallback block (lines ~2819-2826). The function should be:

  fn resolve_adapter(&self) -> Result<crate::dispatch::AgentAdapter> {
      let adapter_name = &self.config.agent.default;
      self.dispatcher.adapter(adapter_name)
          .cloned()
          .ok_or_else(|| anyhow::anyhow!(
              "configured agent adapter '{}' not found — check ~/.needle/agents/{}.yaml or ~/.local/bin/claude-config/agents/{}/config.json exists",
              adapter_name, adapter_name, adapter_name
          ))
  }

The last-resort first-built-in block should also be removed (or kept only if the adapter_name is literally empty/unset, which config validation should prevent).

## Complete when
- resolve_adapter returns Err (not Ok(claude-sonnet)) when config.agent.default is not in dispatcher
- Existing test resolve_adapter_returns_builtin updated to expect Err when adapter not found
- cargo test passes (push to main and wait for needle-ci)

src/cli/mod.rs:64 documents the flag as:

    /// Workspace to process beads from.

That reads as "this worker will only work this repo". It is not what the flag
does, and the wording actively misleads operators into believing --workspace and
roaming are mutually exclusive fleet modes.

What it actually does: --workspace sets the worker HOME workspace only. It does
NOT populate strands.explore.workspaces. With that list empty (the default),
ExploreStrand stays in auto-discovery mode and calls
discover_workspaces(&config.workspace_root), scanning every directory containing
a .beads/ under workspace_root. So a worker launched with -w still reaches every
other repo. Per src/strand/explore.rs:

    - If config.workspaces is empty     -> discover_workspaces(&config.workspace_root)
    - If config.workspaces is non-empty -> uses the explicit list directly

Consequence observed 2026-08-07: an operator concluded that "different repos" and
"roaming" were mutually exclusive and proposed a false choice between them,
because the help text was the only documentation of the flag. Launching 8 workers
with no -w sent all 8 to workspace.default (/home/coding/aide-de-camp), a repo
with ZERO ready beads, while spaxel (59), NEEDLE (48), HOOP (25) and ARMOR (21)
sat unstaffed. The correct launch is one worker per repo with -w, which spreads
the home workspaces AND keeps explore discovery.

Suggested wording:

    /// Home workspace for this worker.
    ///
    /// NOT an exclusive scope. The Explore strand still auto-discovers every
    /// directory containing `.beads/` under `strands.explore.workspace_root`,
    /// so the worker can claim beads in other repos. Setting this only changes
    /// where the worker starts and which store is its home.
    ///
    /// To genuinely restrict a worker to a fixed set of repos, set
    /// `strands.explore.workspaces` instead (a non-empty list disables
    /// auto-discovery). Defaults to `workspace.default`.

Also worth documenting the same distinction in the README / docs, since the
help text is currently the only place the flag is described.

NOTE: not fixed directly because a worker was live in this repo at filing time;
per the workspace rule on live-edit collisions, source changes here go through a
bead rather than a hand-edit.
